content.text:
Yo, listen up.

**Dependency Injection in C#** is when your class stops going out on the street to get its own stuff, and instead, somebody hands it what it needs.

### Without dependency injection

Your class is acting like a lone wolf:

```csharp
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly EmailService _emailService;

    public OrderService()
    {
        _emailService = new EmailService();
    }

    public void PlaceOrder()
    {
        // order logic
        _emailService.SendEmail("Order placed!");
    }
}
```

This class is saying:

> “I don’t need nobody. I’ll make my own EmailService.”

Problem is, now `OrderService` is tightly tied to `EmailService`. You wanna swap in a text message service? A fake service for testing? Too bad. It’s locked in.

That’s bad business.

---

## With dependency injection

Now we do it like a proper organized operation:

```csharp
public interface INotificationService
{
    void Send(string message);
}

public class EmailService : INotificationService
{
    public void Send(string message)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Email sent: {message}");
    }
}

public class OrderService
{
    private readonly INotificationService _notificationService;

    public OrderService(INotificationService notificationService)
    {
        _notificationService = notificationService;
    }

    public void PlaceOrder()
    {
        // order logic
        _notificationService.Send("Order placed!");
    }
}
```

Now `OrderService` ain’t making its own notification guy. It just says:

> “I need someone who can send messages.”

And the outside world says:

> “No problem, boss. Here’s an `EmailService`.”

That’s **dependency injection**.

---

## The setup in ASP.NET Core

You register the crew:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<INotificationService, EmailService>();
builder.Services.AddScoped<OrderService>();
```

Now when the app needs an `OrderService`, the DI container is like:

> “Hold up, this guy needs an `INotificationService`. I know a guy.”

Then it builds everything for you.

---

## Why it matters

Dependency injection helps you:

- **Avoid tight coupling**  
  Your class doesn’t depend on one specific concrete class.

- **Make testing easier**  
  You can inject a fake service.

- **Swap implementations easily**  
  Email today, SMS tomorrow, carrier pigeon next week.

- **Keep your code cleaner**  
  Classes focus on their own job instead of creating all their helpers.

---

## Testing example

You can slide in a fake notification service:

```csharp
public class FakeNotificationService : INotificationService
{
    public void Send(string message)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Fake send: {message}");
    }
}
```

Then test like this:

```csharp
var fakeService = new FakeNotificationService();
var orderService = new OrderService(fakeService);

orderService.PlaceOrder();
```

No real email gets sent. No mess. No heat.

---

## The gangster summary

Without DI:

> “I make my own connections.”

With DI:

> “My connections get brought to me.”

Dependency injection is basically:

> **Don’t let your classes build their own crew. Hand them the crew they need.**

Clean. Flexible. Testable.

That’s the business.

